# SYNOPSIS

```usage
procfs
```

# DESCRIPTION

`procfs` serves `/proc`, the system's one process table (ADR-0011), with 9front's files and the debug files dbg(1) uses.
svcd(8) starts it from `boot/svc/procfs.ndb` and posts it as `/srv/proc`; a namespace that wants the processes mounts `/srv/proc` on `/proc`, as the console shell's manifest and `/lib/ns/posix` do.
svcd gives it a handle to svcd's own task (`tasks`), a connection to nsd(4) for each process's namespace, and one to tmpfs(4) for crash directories.
svcd starts it again when it exits.

The root holds a directory for each process, named by its process id in decimal, listed in the order of procfs's table, not by id.
A pid is the kernel's id of the process's task, which is never reused; exec keeps the task, and so the pid.
Every file is owned by `proc`, and opening one is allowed or refused by its mode alone; an open with `ORCLOSE` is refused.

A process is in `/proc` only once it is registered, and leaves it when its task ends.
Whoever spawns a process registers it before it runs, with a call on the channel procfs listens on, the post `/srv/proc` or the connector a namespace mounted `/proc` from (`lib/vx-proc/proc.h`).
The call carries a handle to the child's task, the parent's pid, the flags `PROC_NOWAIT` (the parent wants no wait record), `PROC_NOTEG` (a note group of its own) and `PROC_SETSID` (a session and a note group of its own), and optionally a note group in the parent's session to join; the reply is the child's pid.
Without either flag a child is in its parent's note group and session.
svcd is the root of the table, admitted through `tasks`; it registers the services it started before procfs, each a note group and session of its own.
procfs holds each task's handle, watches for its end, and catches each fault that nothing else takes ({crash directories|#crash}).

Each process directory holds these files, described in the nodes that follow:

| File | Mode | Holds |
| `status` | r | the process's state, one record |
| `ctl` | w | control verbs |
| `note` | w | a note to post to it |
| `notepg` | w | a note to post to its note group |
| `noteid` | rw | its note group |
| `ppid` | r | its parent's pid |
| `wait` | r | its children's wait records |
| `ns` | r | its namespace |
| `events` | r | debug events |
| `mem` | rw | its address space |
| `maps` | r | its mappings |
| `images` | r | its program's ELF image |
| `info` | r | what the debugger can do on this machine |
| `prof/` | | profiling zones |
| `threads/` | | a directory for each thread |

@node=files title="The process files"

: `status`
  One ndb record (see ndb(6)), made when it is read: `pid`, `name` (the task's name), `state`, `threads` (the number of threads), `mem` (memory mapped into its address space, in KiB, followed by `K`) and `sid` (its session).
  `state` is `new` if the task has not started, `stopped` if `ctl`'s `stop` or a `SIGSTOP` stopped it, `waiting` if every thread is blocked, and `running` otherwise.
  ps(1) prints these records.
: `note`
  A write posts its text, without trailing newlines and spaces, as a note: the kernel interrupts the process with it (see {notes|#notes}).
  It must be 1 to 128 bytes.
: `notepg`
  A write posts the note to every process in this process's note group, the writer included if it is a member, as each `note` would.
  It succeeds even if some of them refuse the note.
: `noteid`
  Reading gives the process's note group, a number in decimal.
  Writing a number moves the process to that group, which must be one some process in its session is in, or its own pid, which makes a new group; any other is refused.
: `ppid`
  The parent's pid, in decimal: the one its registration named, or 0 for svcd.
  A parent that ends leaves its children's `ppid` as it was.
: `ns`
  The namespace(6) text of the namespace group the process is in (ADR-0009), which procfs asks nsd for: what ns(1) would print in it.
  It is empty if the process is in no group or nsd does not answer within a second.
: `wait`
  A read waits for one of the process's children to end, then returns its wait record, one record each read whatever the offset, as in 9front.
  The record holds `pid`, `name`, `noteid` (its note group when it ended), `status` (its exit string, empty for success) and `real` (milliseconds from its registration to its end).
  A process that has asked for `childnotes` (see {control|#ctl}) also gets records of its children's stops, with `stopped=` and the signal in place of `status` and `real`, and of their continues, with the flag `continued`.
  A read with no record queued and no living child that will leave one fails with `no living children`.
  The file's length in a stat is the number of records queued, so a length above 0 means a read will not wait.

A child leaves a record only if its parent is registered and alive and the child was not registered with `PROC_NOWAIT`.
At most 128 records are queued for one parent and 2048 for all of them; past that a child's end goes unrecorded.
A process's unread records go when it ends.

@node=ctl title="Control"

A write to `ctl` is one verb with its arguments; trailing newlines and spaces are dropped.
An address is decimal or hexadecimal after `0x`; a thread id, a signal and a hit count are decimal.
A verb that is not one of these fails with `bad message`.
svcd, the root, refuses every verb that would stop, kill or change it with `permission denied`: the system needs it.

: `kill`
  Ends the process at once, with the exit string `killed`.
: `stop [<sig>]`
  Stops every thread of the process.
  <sig>, from 1 to 64 and `SIGSTOP` (19) when left out, is the signal its parent's wait record and `SIGCHLD` note report.
  Stopping a process already stopped does nothing.
: `start`
  Lets go every thread held at a debug event, and continues a stopped process; its parent hears that it continued.
  While an event is waiting to be read from `events`, it lets nothing go (M6 step 6d6a): a debugger that continues after one thread's stop reads the stop another thread made at the same time first, as gdb reports one before resuming.
: `setsid`
  Gives the process a session and a note group of its own, both named by its pid.
  It is refused with `permission denied` if any process's note group is already that pid, as POSIX refuses a group leader.
: `childnotes`
  From now on the process is sent the note `posix: SIGCHLD pid=<pid>` when a child ends, stops or continues, and gets wait records for stops and continues.
  It cannot be turned off.
: `break <addr> [if <cond>] [after <n>]`
  Sets a breakpoint at <addr> (see {debugging|#debug}).
  `after` lets <n> hits go by before the first that stops.
  <cond>, written without spaces, is a register name (as in `regs.ndb`) or an address in brackets, `[0x7f001000]`, for the 64-bit word there, one of `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`, and a number; the comparison is unsigned.
  A breakpoint at an address that has one replaces its condition and count.
: `unbreak <addr>`
  Removes the breakpoint at <addr>, putting back the code it replaced.
: `watch <addr> <len> write|rw`
  Sets a watchpoint on the <len> bytes at <addr>, stopping a thread that writes them, or (`rw`) reads or writes them.
  A watchpoint at an address that has one replaces it.
  The hardware decides which lengths and alignments it takes; one it refuses leaves the slot as it was.
: `unwatch <addr>`
  Removes the watchpoint at <addr>.
: `step <tid>`
  Runs thread <tid>, which must be held at a debug event, for one instruction; it stops again with `event=step`.
: `freeze <tid>`, `thaw <tid>`
  Suspends thread <tid>, so its registers can be read, or lets it run again.
: `detach`
  Removes every breakpoint and watchpoint, lets every held thread go, and stops taking the process's exceptions first.

@node=notes title="Notes and signals"

A note posted through `note` or `notepg` is delivered by the kernel, which interrupts the process with it (ADR-0010).
If the process is blocked in a read of its own `wait` file, that read ends with `interrupted`, after the note, so the process sees the note first.

The POSIX back end builds signals on notes (`lib/vx-posix/posix.h`), and procfs carries out the signals a process cannot act on itself.
A note naming `SIGKILL` kills the process with `killed`; one naming `SIGSTOP` stops it, as `ctl`'s `stop` does; one naming `SIGCONT` continues it and is then delivered, for its handler.
svcd takes no note at all: it has no handler, and a note would end it, and the system with it.

Unlike 9front's, `notepg` includes the writer: a note to oneself is delivered before the write returns, so POSIX's `kill(0, sig)` signals the caller too.

@node=debug title="Debugging"

The debug files (`docs/05-debugger.md` §3) let a debugger stop and examine a process with reads and writes, as acid reads Plan 9's `/proc`.
Breakpoints live in procfs, not in the debugger, so a shell script and dbg(1) share them.

The first time a breakpoint or watchpoint is set, or `events` is opened, procfs binds its port to the task's exceptions first, before the program's own handler.
procfs then writes the trap instruction (`int3` on x86_64, `brk #0` on aarch64) with `task_mem_rw`, which gives the task a private copy of the code page.
A thread that reaches a breakpoint stays stopped, with an event, until `ctl`'s `start` or its own `resume` or `step`.
procfs then puts the code back, steps the thread over it and puts the trap back.
A breakpoint whose condition is false, or that is within its `after` count, is stepped over at once, with no event and no round trip to the debugger.

When a thread stops with an event, procfs freezes every other thread of the process, each with its own `thread_suspend`, so a `freeze` the debugger made outlasts it, and thaws them when the stopped thread is let go (M6 step 6d6a: all-stop).
A `step` of the stopped thread leaves the others frozen.
While any thread steps over a breakpoint or a watchpoint, with its trap out, the others are frozen too, even for a breakpoint whose condition let it go on, so none runs past it meanwhile.
A thread frozen so that stops at an exception of its own meanwhile is thawed, held at it with an event.
Any number of threads are followed.
A fault stops the faulting thread with an event; letting it go passes the fault on to the program's handler, else to procfs's crash handler.
A trap instruction the program has itself stops the thread with `event=trap`, and letting it go passes it to the program in the same way.
A note is passed on to the program without stopping it.

: `events`
  Opening it makes the opener a debugger, as above.
  A read waits for an event, then returns its record, one each read.
  At most 32 events wait to be read; later ones are lost.
  The file's length in a stat is the number waiting.

An event's record holds `event`, `thread` and `pc`, and for a fault or a watchpoint `addr` and `access`:

```text
event=break thread=1 pc=0x401a20
event=step thread=1 pc=0x401a24
event=fault thread=1 pc=0x401a30 addr=0x0 access=read
event=trap thread=1 pc=0x401a40
event=watch thread=1 pc=0x401a50 addr=0x4c2008 access=write
```

A fault's `access` is `read`, `write` or `exec`; a watchpoint's is the kind it was set as.

: `mem`
  The address space as a file, the offset being the address.
  A read stops at the first page that is not mapped, and a read starting at one fails with `bad message`.
  A write goes a page at a time, and stops at the first page that cannot be written; it may be up to 16 KiB.
  svcd's memory cannot be written.
: `maps`
  One record for each mapping, in address order: `base`, `size`, `prot` (such as `r-x`) and `offset` into its VMO, the numbers in hexadecimal.
: `images`
  One record for the program's ELF image: `name` (the task's name), `base` (the lowest mapping that starts with an ELF header) and `build-id`, in hexadecimal, if the image has a GNU build-id note.
: `info`
  One record: `arch` (`x86_64` or `aarch64`), `watchpoints` (how many the hardware has) and `breakpoints` (how many procfs keeps for a process, 32).
: `threads/<tid>/`
  A directory for each living thread, named by its id in decimal, holding:
: `threads/<tid>/status`
  `state` (`running`, `blocked`, `stopped` or `frozen`), `reason` (`break`, `step`, `fault`, `trap` or `watch`) if procfs holds it at an event, and `pc`, if its registers can be read.
: `threads/<tid>/regs`
  The general registers, binary: the architecture's `vx_regs`.
  It is read, or written, whole and at offset 0.
: `threads/<tid>/regs.ndb`
  The same registers as one record, each in hexadecimal: `rax` to `r15`, `rip` and `rflags` on x86_64; `x0` to `x30`, `sp`, `pc` and `pstate` on aarch64.
  Writing `<name>=<value> ...` sets those registers and leaves the rest.
: `threads/<tid>/fpregs`
  The floating-point and SIMD registers, binary: the architecture's `vx_fpregs`, read or written whole at offset 0.
: `threads/<tid>/xregs`
  All of the thread's FP/SIMD state, binary, as thread(2)'s `VX_STATE_GET_XSTATE` gives it: on x86_64 XSAVE's standard image, AVX and AVX-512 included; read at any offset, written whole at offset 0.
: `threads/<tid>/ctl`
  `step`, `resume` (lets the thread go from its event), `freeze` or `thaw`, for this thread.
: `threads/<tid>/sched`
  The thread's scheduling, one record (sched(2), M6 step 6d6c): its `intent`; `context=yes` if it is bound to a scheduling context, with a realtime one's `period` and `budget` in nanoseconds, the budget `left` this period, and the periods it was `exhausted` in; the context's `reserved` CPUs, a mask, and how many (`cores`); and the CPU it is bound to (`core`), if any; and, while it serves a `channel_call` on the caller's scheduling (6d6c2), the caller's task and thread (`lent_task`, `lent_thread`), whose intent and context the rest then are.

A thread's registers can be read only while it holds still: stopped at an event, or frozen.

@node=prof title="Profiling zones"

A process that calls `vx_prof_init` (`lib/vx-prof/prof.h`) gives procfs its profiling ring, a VMO, with a `PROC_PROF` call on the listen channel, naming where it maps it.
procfs maps the ring too, writes a nonce of its own into it, and takes it only if the process's memory at that address shows the nonce, the proof that the process maps that VMO there.
The VMO holds a ring for each thread (M6 step 6d6b): the first 32 threads that time a zone claim one each and write it alone, sharing no counter; any after them share one more, counted atomically.

: `prof/ctl`
  `zones on` or `zones off` turns the process's zones on or off.
  It fails with `file does not exist` if the process has given no ring.
: `prof/zones`
  The rings as they are now: a `vx_prof_header`, whose `head` and `cap` say how many records follow, then every thread's records merged oldest first by their end, each naming its thread's id.
  Its times are in cycles of the counter sysfs(4) describes in `/sys/clock/info`.

`prof` is listed in the process's directory, and its two files in it.

@node=crash title="Crash directories"

procfs binds every process's exception port last in line, so a fault that neither a debugger nor the program's handler takes reaches it (`docs/05-debugger.md` §5).
It stops the process's other threads and saves the process as a directory shaped like a `/proc` directory, which dbg(1) opens as it opens a live process:

```text
/tmp/crash/NAME.PID/
    info status maps images    as /proc/N's, at the fault
    note                       the fault in Plan 9's words
    threads/T/status regs regs.ndb fpregs xregs
    mem/0xBASE                 each writable mapping's bytes
```

The directory is made on tmpfs, which namespaces mount on `/tmp`, so it is seen as `/tmp/crash/<name>.<pid>`.
Read-only mappings are not copied, since `images` names them by build id; of the writable ones at most 16 MiB is saved, and a mapping's file ends at its first page that cannot be read.
Each call to tmpfs has a time limit of 5 seconds, since the process that faulted may be tmpfs or one it waits on.
procfs then prints a line on the console and kills the process with the fault's note as its exit string, as an unhandled fault always ends it.

A fault of procfs's own is not caught, since it would wait for procfs to take it: procfs ends, and svcd starts it again.

# EXAMPLES

The manifest that starts it, `boot/svc/procfs.ndb`:

```ndb
service=procfs program=/boot/bin/procfs post=proc tasks console restart
connect=nsd
connect=tmpfs
```

From the shell test, svcd's status and a kill:

```rc
% cat /proc/1/status
pid=1 name=svcd state=waiting threads=1 mem=32560K sid=1
% uartpid=2; echo kill > /proc/$uartpid/ctl
```

after which svcd reports `svcd: uart exited: killed` and starts it again.

A crash, from the debugger test: procfs prints

`procfs: dbgdemo.17: sys: trap: fault read addr=0x10 pc=0x21afa0; saved in /tmp/crash`

and dbg(1), which ran the program, then says:

```text
dbg: dbgdemo exited: sys: trap: fault read addr=0x10 pc=0x21afa0
dbg: crash directory /tmp/crash/dbgdemo.17
```

# FILES

: `/srv/proc`
  procfs's post, and the channel processes are registered on.
: `/proc/<pid>/`
  A process.
: `/tmp/crash/<name>.<pid>/`
  A crash directory.

